WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.650 --> 00:00:03.230
They're Earth's Mightiest Heroes.

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:05.520
But how much do you really know about the
Avengers?

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:08.509
Here are 10 Avengers facts you may not know.

00:00:08.509 --> 00:00:09.870
What's in a name?

00:00:09.870 --> 00:00:13.969
When you think of the Avengers, you probably
think of heroes like Iron Man, Thor, Black

00:00:13.969 --> 00:00:15.530
Widow, and Captain America.

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:19.070
But it was another superhero who actually
gave the team its name—a character that

00:00:19.070 --> 00:00:22.630
still hasn't even appeared in the movies except
in one little flashback:

00:00:22.630 --> 00:00:27.220
That's right Janet van Dyne, the Wasp, was
a founding member of the Avengers in the comics,

00:00:27.220 --> 00:00:28.679
and even came up with the name, too.

00:00:28.679 --> 00:00:31.349
That Nick Fury's always trying to take credit
for everything:

00:00:31.349 --> 00:00:34.500
"I'm here to talk to you about the Avenger
Initiative."

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:35.500
Wasps for Widows

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:38.570
While she may be missing from the team she
named, there was a point in time where the

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:42.370
Wasp would've made her cinematic debut in
the original Avengers movie.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:46.730
Writer/director Joss Whedon explained in a
Q&amp;A session after a screening of the movie

00:00:46.730 --> 00:00:49.530
that he'd written the Wasp into the early
drafts of the screenplay:

00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:53.140
"There was a little bit of time where we thought
we might not actually get Scarlett [Johansson]

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:55.350
There was a very Waspy draft that I wrote.

00:00:55.350 --> 00:00:56.580
But it was way too Waspy.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:58.010
I was like, ‘She’s adorable!

00:00:58.010 --> 00:00:59.690
I’m just going to watch her!'”

00:00:59.690 --> 00:01:03.420
Eventually Johansson did sign up, bringing
the Black Widow back to the screen...and the

00:01:03.420 --> 00:01:04.420
Wasp got squashed.

00:01:04.420 --> 00:01:05.790
Good, I hate wasps...

00:01:05.790 --> 00:01:08.680
Hulk smash...membership card

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:12.190
In the movies, the Hulk is one of the most
high-profile members of the team.

00:01:12.190 --> 00:01:16.400
However, he was only an Avenger for a single
issue in the comics: they formed in the last

00:01:16.400 --> 00:01:21.200
panel of Avengers #1, and by the end of the
second issue, he'd already quit in a rage.

00:01:21.200 --> 00:01:22.520
What a baby.

00:01:22.520 --> 00:01:27.340
A few issues later, a recently unfrozen Captain
America replaced the Hulk, and the rest is

00:01:27.340 --> 00:01:28.730
superhero history.

00:01:28.730 --> 00:01:30.310
Kang, outfoxed

00:01:30.310 --> 00:01:34.640
As the second movie in the franchise shows,
Ultron is one of the most important Avengers

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:35.640
villains.

00:01:35.640 --> 00:01:40.320
But another nemesis stands out as their all-time
biggest threat: Kang the Conqueror, a time-traveling

00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.380
warlord who has menaced the team since 1964.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:46.160
So why hasn't he appeared in an MCU film yet?

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:50.760
Because even though he made his debut as an
Avengers bad guy, he's also closely associated

00:01:50.760 --> 00:01:52.190
with the Fantastic Four.

00:01:52.190 --> 00:01:56.170
And according to Guardians of the Galaxy's
director James Gunn, Marvel included the film

00:01:56.170 --> 00:02:00.320
rights to Kang as part of their Fantastic
Four contract with 20th Century Fox.

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.500
As a result, Kang is probably off the MCU
table for good.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:08.830
Avengers disassembled

00:02:08.830 --> 00:02:13.139
One of the most infamous moments in Avengers
history took place in issue #16, in which

00:02:13.139 --> 00:02:17.889
the entire original team quit, only to be
replaced by reformed supervillains.

00:02:17.889 --> 00:02:19.580
Why such a drastic roster change?

00:02:19.580 --> 00:02:24.900
Well, because Iron Man, Thor, Giant-Man and
the Wasp were starring in their own solo comics,

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.120
and Marvel Mastermind Stan Lee was getting
confused about the continuity.

00:02:28.120 --> 00:02:29.450
"Did it work?"

00:02:29.450 --> 00:02:33.370
Rather than spend time figuring out each month
when each adventure happened in relation to

00:02:33.370 --> 00:02:37.290
the others, he decided to just feature different
characters who weren't appearing in other

00:02:37.290 --> 00:02:38.290
comics.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:39.650
Avengers: Age of Makino

00:02:39.650 --> 00:02:43.180
If you've ever looked at Ultron and thought
he was pretty well-suited to be an evil robot,

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:48.069
you can thank a comic book called Captain
Video — specifically issue number 3 from

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:49.069
1951.

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:53.489
According to an interview with Ultron's co-creator
Roy Thomas, artist John Buscema was partially

00:02:53.489 --> 00:02:56.629
inspired by the villain, Makino's, creepy
design.

00:02:56.629 --> 00:02:59.389
Just take a look at that perma-smile on his
face...

00:02:59.389 --> 00:03:00.870
Visions of other worlds

00:03:00.870 --> 00:03:04.889
Speaking of writer Roy Thomas, he'd asked
Stan Lee if he could add a new member to the

00:03:04.889 --> 00:03:06.109
Avengers in 1968.

00:03:06.109 --> 00:03:10.959
He reportedly wanted to resurrect the long-forgotten
Golden Age superhero character who first appeared

00:03:10.959 --> 00:03:16.980
in Marvel Comics #13 back in 1940: an alien
known as the Vision, an alien cop from a smoke

00:03:16.980 --> 00:03:19.870
dimension who comes to Earth to battle mystical
crime.

00:03:19.870 --> 00:03:24.230
But Stan Lee had other plans, asking that
the new addition be a robot of some kind.

00:03:24.230 --> 00:03:27.959
So Thomas and Buscema simply took the name
and design for Vision and used it to make

00:03:27.959 --> 00:03:29.419
a new, robotic version.

00:03:29.419 --> 00:03:31.400
And this one could even cry.

00:03:31.400 --> 00:03:32.620
Comic book casting

00:03:32.620 --> 00:03:34.199
Follow along with this one, if you can.

00:03:34.199 --> 00:03:35.700
"Hold on to your butts."

00:03:35.700 --> 00:03:40.150
In the comics, Nick Fury was originally a
white World War II vet who eventually became

00:03:40.150 --> 00:03:41.340
an Agent of SHIELD.

00:03:41.340 --> 00:03:45.819
When Marvel updated the character in the early
2000s for their “Ultimate” universe, artist

00:03:45.819 --> 00:03:50.480
Bryan Hitch decided to use Samuel L. Jackson
as the model for the new version of Nick Fury.

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.499
So when Marvel Studios produced the first
Iron Man movie back in 2008, and the post-credits

00:03:55.499 --> 00:04:00.029
scene called for a cameo from Fury, there
was only one man for the job...Samuel L. Jackson:

00:04:00.029 --> 00:04:02.730
"Who the hell are you?"

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:04.829
"Nick Fury, Director of SHIELD."

00:04:04.829 --> 00:04:08.809
Once the movies became a hit, Marvel decided
to make the comic book Nick Fury in the main

00:04:08.809 --> 00:04:10.680
Marvel Universe match the films.

00:04:10.680 --> 00:04:15.219
To explain the change, the original Fury got
a previously unknown African-American son,

00:04:15.219 --> 00:04:19.659
appropriately named Nick Fury, Jr., who took
over running SHIELD for dear old dad.

00:04:19.659 --> 00:04:23.860
These days, Nick Senior is out of the picture...because
he's been chained to the moon.

00:04:23.860 --> 00:04:25.160
"How's that workin' out for ya?"

00:04:25.160 --> 00:04:27.229
Rogue was an Avengers villain

00:04:27.229 --> 00:04:32.509
Back in 1981's Avengers Annual #10, the heroes
faced off against the Brotherhood of Evil

00:04:32.509 --> 00:04:36.400
Mutants, which boasted a new member: a superpower-stealing
villain named Rogue.

00:04:36.400 --> 00:04:39.260
Two years later, she switched sides and joined
the X-Men.

00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:42.759
Since then, she's become one of the most famous
members of the mutant superteam...though,

00:04:42.759 --> 00:04:45.739
that's partially because of her southern,
uh, "charm."

00:04:46.000 --> 00:04:50.100
"You're as fast as a swamp bug on a hot skillet."

00:04:50.100 --> 00:04:52.490
Joss Whedon, superhero scribe

00:04:52.490 --> 00:04:57.270
On the subject of Marvelous mutants, Avengers
writer/director Joss Whedon actually had the

00:04:57.270 --> 00:05:00.430
opportunity to take a crack at the mutants'
first big-screen adventure.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:01.660
He told the A.V.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:05.340
Club that he'd been invited to punch up the
script for the first X-Men movie.

00:05:05.340 --> 00:05:06.340
So what happened?

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:07.340
Whedon explained:

00:05:07.340 --> 00:05:10.509
"[...] not only did they throw out my script
and never tell me about it; they actually

00:05:10.509 --> 00:05:14.849
invited me to the read-through, having thrown
out my entire draft without telling me."

00:05:14.849 --> 00:05:18.569
Apparently, however, one line he wrote did
make it into the final film:

00:05:18.569 --> 00:05:20.220
"Hey, hey...it's me."

00:05:20.220 --> 00:05:21.319
"Prove it."

00:05:21.319 --> 00:05:22.970
"You're a dick."

00:05:22.970 --> 00:05:27.400
Meanwhile, a few years later, Warner Brothers
invited Whedon to write and direct a Wonder

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:30.669
Woman feature film...but the project was scrapped
in 2007.

00:05:30.669 --> 00:05:36.490
Incredibly, Whedon's Avengers movie in 2012
earned over $1.5 billion and made the Marvel

00:05:36.490 --> 00:05:39.110
Cinematic Universe the envy of Hollywood.

00:05:39.110 --> 00:05:43.400
For Avengers fans, there's only one thing
to say to 20th Century Fox and Warner Bros.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:44.400
for their mistakes:

00:05:44.400 --> 00:05:45.400
"Good move."

00:05:45.400 --> 00:05:46.400
Thanks for watching!

00:05:46.400 --> 00:05:49.360
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:05:49.360 --> 00:05:53.729
And leave us a comment to let us know which
Avengers fact surprised you the most.

